610:        Background and scenario    A client runs its sales team in Salesforce, a customer relationship management (CRM) product. Tequity has built the client an operations portal. Finance and customer-success staff work in the portal.  The same people exist in both places, as customers and as contacts. A sales person creates a customer in Salesforce. The portal should show that customer. A finance person on the portal then corrects the billing city. Salesforce should show the new city. Later, a sales person changes the phone number in Salesforce. The portal should show the new phone, and the city the finance person set should still be there.  The client also needs the history. They need to know who changed the phone, and who changed the city. The fact of an earlier edit stays on record after a later edit.  Salesforce stores its own copy. The portal stores its copy in PostgreSQL (Postgres). The two copies have to stay in sync. We are designing and building our side. Salesforce already exists, and it stays as it is.    
611:        The systems involved    Three parties matter.     Salesforce.  The client's CRM. Sales users edit records here. Salesforce has its own database.     The portal.  Our application. Finance and customer-success users edit records here. Our database is Postgres.     The network between them.  Salesforce and the portal talk over the public internet, using Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure (HTTPS). Each side keeps its own database. HTTPS is the only path between them.    Who calls whom:    When a record changes in Salesforce, Salesforce calls us. The call is a webhook: an HTTPS request to an endpoint we host. The webhook names the record and describes the change.    When a record changes in the portal, we call Salesforce. The call uses Salesforce's official record application programming interface (API): the create and update operations Salesforce already documents. The portal reaches Salesforce through that record API.    A sales user talks only to Salesforce. A portal user talks only to the portal. Neither user talks to the other system.    The rate limit below applies to the requests we send to Salesforce. Webhooks Salesforce sends to us sit outside that cap.     Sales user                         Finance / customer-success user
