629:           create or update record    The webhook endpoint and the sync worker are both ours. They can be one service or two. Say which you choose, and what crosses the boundary between them.  A webhook is a message. Our endpoint has to accept it, check it, and apply it before anything is stored. A call to the Salesforce record API is a request we initiate. Salesforce may accept it, reject it, or be unreachable.    
630:        Requirements and scale    Records:    Customers and contacts. About these fields, and no others, for this exercise: customer name, status, and owner; contact full name, phone, email, city, owner, and the customer they belong to.     5 million records  in production, customers and contacts counted together.    Sync:    While both systems are up, a create or an update on either side is visible on the other side within  30 seconds . Thirty seconds is the worst case. Everyday sync should land sooner.    Both sides may create. Both sides may update.    The current value of a field is the one we can justify. The earlier values, who made them, and when, stay on record. Dropping that history counts as losing a change.    An update we send to Salesforce must not bounce back and forth forever.    Webhooks:    Salesforce may deliver the same webhook twice.    Salesforce may deliver webhooks in a different order from the order of the edits.    Each webhook carries at least the fields in the sample below.  occurredAt  is the time Salesforce gives the event.    The CRM API allows at most  100 requests per second . That is our cap on calls to Salesforce. We choose how far under the cap to run.  We left the peak edit rate open. State the rate you assume, in edits per second, and use it in the arithmetic.  Sample webhook. This is enough to start. You may add fields. Say why.    {
