647:        Constraints     Rate limit.  At most 100 requests per second to the Salesforce record API. The cap counts the calls we make, including retries. Webhooks arriving at our endpoint sit outside this cap. If Salesforce tells us to slow down, we slow down, and we keep the work.   Outages.  Design for the Salesforce API being unreachable for  6 hours . Webhooks Salesforce tried to send during that window may never arrive. Pending calls to Salesforce must still be there after our own process restarts.  Thirty seconds applies while both systems are up. Catch-up after an outage is a different target. Drain the backlog as fast as the rate limit allows. Compute how long that takes, in minutes, for the edit rate you assumed. Show the division.   Consistency.  Two people may edit the same record at the same time, on the same side or on opposite sides. Edits to different fields of that record both have to survive. Edits to the same field need one current value and a kept history.  Say what you use to decide which edit is current, on a webhook and on a portal save. Name the field. If you need an API this note does not give you, mark it as an assumption.   Out of scope for this weekend:  deletes, Salesforce login screens, a visual user interface, a second region, and a load test against real Salesforce. If you write a program, call a fake CRM you write yourself.    
648:        What you deliver    Three things are required. A program is optional.   We   ask   for   the   chats   and   the   commits   because   we   want   to   see   how   you   prompt   the   artificial   intelligence   (AI)   assistant,   and   how   your   thinking   develops.   The   final   document   alone   does   not   show   that.     1. The design document  This is the main piece, a few pages, long enough for the rules and the arithmetic. Write it so a teammate who was not on the call can build from it.  These four have to be in it, with a rule for each:    a duplicate webhook    a change that arrives out of order    a sync loop, where our own update comes back    the 6-hour Salesforce outage, including the minutes of arithmetic    Use this outline:    Assumptions, and the questions you would ask before you lock the design.    The systems, who calls whom, the network boundary, and the interface on each side.    The subproblems, in the order you would build them. Name the subproblems first. Add a component only when a subproblem needs it.    The data you store.    The inbound path, from webhook to our database.    The outbound path, from a portal edit to a Salesforce API call.    The rule when both sides change the same record.    The rule for a duplicate webhook, and for an older webhook that arrives after a newer one.    The rule that stops a loop.    The 6-hour outage: what is on disk, how you drain it, and the minutes of arithmetic.    Three numbers you would watch, and the value at which you would call someone.    What you are leaving out of this weekend's work.        2. Your full AI chat history, unedited  Include every chat you used for this task, every prompt, and every reply.  Do not clean the chats up, trim them, or rewrite them. Leave the wrong turns in. We will read them as they happened.  A single prompt that says "build the sync", followed by an answer you did not question, is a weak submission.      3. Your git repository, with every commit  Send the repository. Keep every commit and its message. Do not squash the work into one commit.  A message that says what you decided is useful. One commit called "final" is not useful.      4. Code, if you have time  Code is optional. Node.js is welcome if you want to check a rule. It is not required, and we will not treat a missing program as a gap.  If you build it, use TypeScript on Node.js, version 20 or newer. It has to run with Node.js alone. Fake Salesforce. One command should run a demo from a fresh checkout.  npm install  plus that command is the upper bound.  The useful checks are the same four rules: a duplicate, an older event after a newer one, a webhook that must not be sent back to Salesforce, and a pending call that is still there after a restart while the fake Salesforce is down. The cap in the design is 100 requests per second. In a demo you may set a lower cap so the wait is visible. The code must enforce the cap it is given.  A service we would ship is out of scope.      
649:        What we will evaluate    We are looking at three things.     How you break the problem into subproblems.  The order should show which part is risky, and why you take that part before the rest.     How you design the system and its components.  The systems, the caller on each path, the network boundary, and the interface on each side should be explicit. The rules for the 6-hour outage, duplicates, out-of-order changes, and sync loops should be specific, and the numbers should close.     How you prompt an AI assistant, and how your thinking develops.  The chats and the commits should show the path from the first question to the final design. You should be able to point at a place you pushed back.    If you bring code, we will check that it matches the design. A rule that is only in the document, and a demo that does something else, will come up on the call. Bringing no code will not come up on the call.    
651:        Assumptions and questions    Put this section at the top of your design document. These are silent in the brief, so they need an assumption or a question:    How many edits per second happen at peak, and how many in a quiet hour?    Does a webhook carry changed fields only, or the whole record?    Can we ask Salesforce for records that changed during the 6 hours, or are those changes gone unless we already have them?    When two portal users edit the same field, what does the other user see, and what do we send Salesforce?    What do we trust for order on a portal edit, given that the sample has no time field?    Add any other question that would change the design. A question you do not have time to answer is still worth writing down.    
